{"arikosWa_(?)":[{"id":"arikozWa_(?)","headword":"अरिकोष्ठ_(?)","headword_slp1":"arikozWa_(?)","senses_r":[{"n":1,"spans":[{"t":"txt","s":"Arikoṣṭha_(?)\njy. Rice 28."}]}]}],"arigraha":[{"id":"arigraha","headword":"अरिग्रह","headword_slp1":"arigraha","senses_r":[{"n":1,"spans":[{"t":"txt","s":"Arigraha\nAv. khila. Kavīndrācārya 97."}]}]}],"aricCit":[{"id":"aricCit","headword":"अरिच्छित्","headword_slp1":"aricCit","senses_r":[{"n":1,"spans":[{"t":"txt","s":"Aricchit\nson of Pravarasena, a Dramiḍa prince, son of king Dramiḍa.\n--C. Sudhāvidyotinī on Saundaryalaharī. which is ascribed to king Pravarasena, the father of the commentator. "},{"t":"cite","s":"MD. 10876","q":"MD"},{"t":"txt","s":". Śg. I. p.. 132-35. "},{"t":"cite","s":"TCD. 293","q":"TCD"},{"t":"txt","s":"F.\nUsually the hymn Saundaryalaharī is ascribed to Śaṅkarācārya; in "},{"t":"cite","s":"MD. 10874","q":"MD"},{"t":"txt","s":", Diṇḍima Rāma in his C. records traditions ascribing it to Śiva Himself an. Devī Herself, besides Śaṅkarācārya.\nIn this C. Sudhāvidyotinī, however, we are given a new story. In the Tamil country there was a. prince of the royal house called Pravarasena, who had been blessed by Devī with her own breast-milk an. who composed this hymn in a forest where his father, [Page370-a. king Dramiḍa had abandoned him soon after his birth owing to some bad combination of planets in his horoscope. This Pravarasena, in previous birth, had been a Brahman on the banks of the Ganges, worshipping Kāmarāja. He was subsequently brought to the capital; to him an. queen Rūpavatī was born Aricchit who wrote his gloss on his father's hymn.\nIn this connection it may be pointed out that the hymn itself refers to the story of a.“Dramiḍa śiśu” being given milk by the Goddess, a. allusion which is usually taken to refer to the Tamil hymnist Jñānasambandhar."}]}]}],"arinigrahakarakartaviryarjunastotra":[{"id":"arinigrahakarakArtavIryArjunastotra","headword":"अरिनिग्रहकरकार्तवीर्यार्जुनस्तोत्र","headword_slp1":